        "theme":  "Orchestration",
        "summary":  "Google\u0027s Antigravity platform, using the Teamwork framework for multi-agent collaboration powered by Gemini 3.7 Flash, solved seven open problems in mathematics and theoretical computer science including Knuth\u0027s Cycles Conjecture. The system also constructed a cycle-accurate RISC-V CPU simulator capable of running operating systems and delivered measurable improvements to widely used open-source libraries. These results demonstrate that autonomous AI teams can sustain progress on complex, long-horizon technical challenges across diverse domains.",
        "execSummary":  "The Antigravity Teamwork results are the most technically credible public demonstration of multi-agent orchestration tackling genuinely hard, long-horizon problems -- not toy benchmarks. For enterprise architects, this validates that agent-team architectures can handle tasks requiring sustained context, iterative reasoning, and cross-domain coordination, which is the profile of complex customer escalations, compliance reviews, and root-cause analyses. The open-source library improvement use case is immediately practical: enterprises with large codebases can apply similar agent-team patterns to technical debt remediation and quality assurance. The caveat is that these achievements required substantial compute and engineering orchestration from Google Research; replicating the approach in a production enterprise environment requires significant platform investment.",
