        "summary":  "Anthropic and HHMI Janelia Research Campus unveiled the Model Hardware Standard (MHS), a specification enabling AI agents to safely operate multiple laboratory and manufacturing instruments simultaneously. MHS reduces hardware integration time from weeks to hours via unified driver software and standard protocols, eliminating the need for custom device integrations. Early adopters in biotech, robotics, and quantum computing have demonstrated significant efficiency gains.",
        "execSummary":  "MHS directly addresses the prohibitive integration cost that has slowed physical AI automation in scientific and industrial environments, compressing deployment timelines from months to hours. By providing a unified interface any programmable device can adopt, it removes the dependency on specialized automation engineers and bespoke code -- a meaningful operating leverage gain for asset-heavy industries. Early results are compelling: Genentech optimized fluid dynamics autonomously, Carnegie Mellon cut experimental duration to one-third, and QuEra achieved 99.3% autonomous laser recovery versus 58% with legacy approaches. Currently a research preview with select partners, broader availability will determine how quickly this translates into enterprise operations outside the lab.",
