        "title":  "AI Security Is an Engineering Problem -- How to Solve It at Every Layer of the Agent Stack",
        "source":  "NVIDIA AI Blog",
        "url":  "https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/ai-security-agent-stack/",
        "date":  "2026-09-21",
        "theme":  "Enterprise",
        "summary":  "NVIDIA argues that AI security requires systematic engineering practices including enforceable controls across all agent stack layers -- models, harnesses, and runtime environments. Organizations must implement identity verification, access restrictions, activity logging, and continuous security testing before deploying AI agents. Open research initiatives and shared defensive tools help the broader security community strengthen AI systems.",
        "execSummary":  "As AI agents become more autonomous, security governance must evolve from isolated model-level guardrails to comprehensive controls spanning the entire architecture -- this piece provides the engineering framework for that shift. The article emphasizes that security controls must operate independently of AI reasoning, meaning they cannot rely on the agent making the right decision under adversarial conditions. For enterprise customer-support leaders, this is directly relevant: autonomous support agents handling sensitive customer data require the same identity, access, and audit trail controls applied to human operators. Adopting frameworks like NVIDIA OpenShell and partnering with security alliances positions organizations to meet evolving compliance requirements while industry standards are still forming.",
